    "q-350036": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350036",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=\\frac{2\\pi x}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
