    "q-13524": {
      "dom_id": "step-q13524",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Calculate the integral ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
