    "q-350055": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350055",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /12$ | $\\pi /9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /4$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =2(2-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =4-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
