    "q-13655": {
      "dom_id": "step-q13655",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
