    "q-350186": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350186",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /4}}8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=4{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=8x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /4}}8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /4}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}^{2})\\cdot 8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
